    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "A bushwalker starts at point $A$ and finishes at point $B$ by walking 10 km east, 9 km north, 4 km west, and 1 km south. Find the straight-line distance from $A$ to $B$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$10\\text{ km}$",
      "worked": "The net displacement is $(10-4)=6\\text{ km}$ east and $(9-1)=8\\text{ km}$ north. Let $d$ kilometres be the straight-line distance.\n$$\\begin{align*}d^2&=6^2+8^2\\\\d^2&=100\\\\d&=10\\end{align*}$$\nThe straight-line distance is $10\\text{ km}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
